  "premises": [
    {
      "id": "premise-1",
      "area": "concurrency",
      "premise": "Every return path of addrConn.resetTransportAndUnlock releases ac.mu exactly once (never leaked, never double-unlocked), and both callers (connect, and the `go` statement in updateAddrs) hand it the lock held with no further ac.mu use after the call.",
      "evidence": [
        {"coordinate": "clientconn.go:921-922", "text": "\tac.resetTransportAndUnlock()\n\treturn nil"},
        {"coordinate": "clientconn.go:994-996", "text": "\t// attempt.\n\tgo ac.resetTransportAndUnlock()\n}"},
        {"coordinate": "clientconn.go:1234-1239", "text": "func (ac *addrConn) resetTransportAndUnlock() {\n\tacCtx := ac.ctx\n\tif acCtx.Err() != nil {\n\t\tac.mu.Unlock()\n\t\treturn\n\t}"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "premise-2",
      "area": "concurrency",
      "premise": "In updateAddrs, ac.mu is now held across the `go ac.resetTransportAndUnlock()` handoff while updateAddrs's deferred `ac.transport.GracefulClose()` runs after updateAddrs returns; this cannot deadlock, because the spawned goroutine never waits on updateAddrs (or its deferred call) before unlocking, and GracefulClose/onClose only take ac.mu (waiting for the goroutine's unlock). Also nothing else the goroutine does before Unlock (updateConnectivityState -> acbw.updateState) blocks on a lock or channel that the updateAddrs caller (acBalancerWrapper.UpdateAddresses, running on the balancer serializer) holds.",
      "evidence": [
        {"coordinate": "clientconn.go:980-996", "text": "\tif ac.transport != nil {\n\t\tdefer ac.transport.GracefulClose()\n\t\tac.transport = nil\n\t}\n\n\tif len(addrs) == 0 {\n\t\tac.updateConnectivityState(connectivity.Idle, nil)\n\t}\n\n\t// Since we were connecting/connected, we should start a new connection\n\t// attempt.\n\tgo ac.resetTransportAndUnlock()"},
        {"coordinate": "balancer_wrapper.go:255-265", "text": "func (acbw *acBalancerWrapper) updateState(s connectivity.State, err error) {\n\tacbw.ccb.serializer.Schedule(func(ctx context.Context) {"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "premise-3",
      "area": "concurrency",
      "premise": "The rule 'at most one connection attempt (tryAllAddrs) is started per Idle->Connecting transition' now holds in steady state: connect() checks state==Idle and resetTransportAndUnlock sets state=Connecting inside one ac.mu critical section, so a second concurrent connect() (or an updateAddrs goroutine) cannot observe Idle after the first has committed to an attempt; the only path that leaves state Idle after early return is the acCtx.Err()!=nil branch, where no attempt is started.",
      "evidence": [
